WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:09.900
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.300
So in this section, we'll
look at tracking detectors.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.600
And before we look at tracking
detector technologies,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.970
we want to remind ourselves
how we measure the momentum

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.415
of a charged particle.

00:00:19.415 --> 00:00:22.590
And this measurement is possible
because charged particles are

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:25.200
reflected in magnetic fields.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.200
We have already seen that in
a homogeneous magnetic field,

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.000
a particle follows a circle.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.950
And so from the
measurement of the radius

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.990
and the knowledge of
the magnetic field,

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:38.310
we can infer the transverse
momentum of the particle.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.980
Typical particles also have
a longitudinal momentum.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:44.520
And so therefore, the
trajectory is actually

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.030
of the form of a helix.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.510
And so we then can get
back to the total momentum

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:52.380
of the particle by knowing
the angle or the component

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.740
of the longitudinal
and transverse momentum

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.860
and then just calculate
total momentum from there.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.650
So we have seen
that particles, when

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:09.820
they go through a piece of
material, that the energy loss

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:12.310
through ionization
or bremsstrahlung.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:15.550
And so what we have to do
now is put some material

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:20.740
in the way of the particles
without really changing

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:24.140
its momentum or
changing its trajectory.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.050
So this is typically done
with tracking detectors.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:31.600
And so what you then measure is
not just the radius directly,

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.720
but you measure
individual points

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.900
that's part of the
trajectory of the particles.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.720
And so from the
measurement of the points,

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:45.860
you can then reconstruct the
trajectory of the particles,

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.210
and then therefore
measure the curvature.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.090
And that has been
used to measure

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:52.920
the transverse momentum.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.440
So if you look at
this picture here,

00:01:54.440 --> 00:02:01.730
the radius can be given as L
squared over 8s plus s half.

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:08.250
s is typically called the
sagitta of the trajectory.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:13.670
If L is much, much larger
than s, it simplifies to r--

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:17.510
about L squared over 8s.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:22.400
And the uncertainty on the
sagitta the uncertainty on s,

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.160
is limiting the uncertainty
on the momentum measurement.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.990
And this is described
in a paper which

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:29.060
is very nice from the '60s.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.750
And the formula is called
the Gluckstern formula.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:37.710
So what you see here is that the
more measurements you actually

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:41.520
perform along the sagitta,
the better your measurement

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:42.190
is performed.

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:43.830
And this goes with
the square root of 1

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.140
over the number of measurements.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:49.930
The total momentum uncertainty,
or the relative momentum

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:52.930
uncertainty, sigma
pt over pt, is

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:56.170
proportional to the
uncertainty in the sagitta.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:59.350
And that also is then
proportional to the pt

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:00.350
of the measurement.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:05.530
So if you want to improve your
measurement of your momentum,

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:07.750
you want to decrease
the uncertainty,

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.670
then you can do this
by having a larger L--

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:14.110
and you see this goes with
the square of the length--

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:20.050
you want to increase
your magnetic field,

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:24.660
and you want to reduce the
uncertainty on the second term.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.790
Those are the elements
you have in play in order

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:29.730
to improve the
transverse momentum,

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:33.510
or the momentum measurement
of your charged particles.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:37.850
So this is a screenshot
of this paper.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:41.360
The measurement error
is not the only error

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.940
on the transverse momentum,
as we discussed previously

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.140
already.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:52.265
Multiple scattering, so the
scattering of the particle--

00:03:52.265 --> 00:03:56.990
and it goes with momentum--
also reduces the uncertainty

00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:58.370
in the momentum measurement.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.410
And we can show
that this component

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:03.510
on the relative momentum
uncertainty is flat.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:07.130
So what you find typically is
that the transverse momentum

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:11.270
measurement is limited by, at
high pt through the measurement

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:14.750
error, and at low pt
through multiple scattering.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:17.820
We have those two components
entering the measurement

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.050
uncertainty.

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:21.750
So the actual detectors,
I just give you

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:24.100
a couple of examples here.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:25.750
The first one--
and the first one,

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:28.800
which actually really has
allowed us to make measurements

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:29.670
with a lot--

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.370
devices with huge
statistic experiments where

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:33.930
you have colliding
beams, and you

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:37.020
look at many, many
of those interactions

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.990
was a multi wire
proportional chamber,

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.460
at a reasonable resolution.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.100
Typically, we have
hundreds or thousands

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.710
of wires with a spacing
of up to 1 millimeter.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:49.680
And this 1-millimeter
spacing in a wire chamber

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:52.285
limits the spatial resolution.

00:04:52.285 --> 00:04:54.660
If you don't have any knowledge
of where the particle was

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:57.750
in that area, then the
uncertainty of the spatial--

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:01.170
on just one hit,
uncertainty is given by d,

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.000
the spacing between
those two wires--

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.780
1 millimeter in this case--

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:07.210
divided by square root of 12.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:10.200
So you find typically
resolutions of 300 microns

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:10.990
per measurement.

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:13.890
But you may gain off the
fact that you typically have

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:15.360
many, many, many measurements.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.580
And as you just saw, this
reduces the uncertainty

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.850
on the measurement.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:21.600
You knowing that there
was a hit on a wire

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.130
gives you two-dimension
information.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:25.780
If you then built
the wire chamber such

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:27.780
that there's angles
between individual wires,

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.210
you can use that
in order to gain

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:34.080
three-dimensional information
in the geometry of where

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.760
the particle went through.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:39.070
Wire chambers can be
operated in different modes.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:43.090
And it depends on the level of
voltage you apply on the wires.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:45.280
So the higher the voltage--

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.980
the level of the
voltage goes down here,

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:52.300
see a very limited voltage
applied, then the ions--

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:54.250
you have ionization,
and the ions

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:56.590
recombine without
being collected.

00:05:56.590 --> 00:06:00.370
In ionization mode,
you collect the ions.

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:02.530
And there's typically
a gain factor of 1.

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:06.370
You measure each ion,
basically, separately.

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:08.920
You can increase the voltage
and run the wire chamber

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:12.160
in proportional mode so you
have a gas multiplication

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.350
factor so the ions
are being accelerated,

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:17.800
and then they produce many
more, more ions to be measured.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:22.030
And you typically have gain
factors of 10 to the fourth.

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:24.550
If you drive this
further up, you

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:28.510
go into the limited proportional
mode, and then in Geiger mode,

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:31.270
where basically you
have an avalanche,

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:34.090
and so you have no information
about the initial ionization

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:34.950
anymore.

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:37.000
And typically, the
chamber breaks down

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:41.100
and needs to recover afterwards.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.740
The signal formation is
quite an interesting concept.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:45.243
So you think that
you do ionization,

00:06:45.243 --> 00:06:47.660
the electrons are there, and
then they're being collected.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:51.830
Yes, that's true, and this
happens very rapidly, typically

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:53.480
in the order of nanoseconds.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:57.600
But the actual real signal
comes from the ions themselves.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:01.100
And so what happens here
is you have your wire here,

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:02.570
you have your electrons.

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:07.310
The ions then themselves, they
build this cloud of charges.

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:13.210
And this cloud of charges then
gives you a signal on the wire.

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:15.740
And this happens
typically via influence,

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:18.950
or you basically have a mirror
charge on the conductor.

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:22.010
And that is really where
the bulk of the information

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:22.510
comes from.

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:28.730
We just discussed that the
spatial resolution is limited

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:30.650
by the spacing of the wires.

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.990
You can actually get
better measurements

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:34.700
by using the timing information.

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:39.170
If you are able to measure the
time profile of your signal,

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:42.410
that profile has information
about the distance

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:45.020
between the ionization
and the wire itself.

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:47.730
And that then helps you to
improve the resolution further.

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:54.720
So I really don't
do justice to this,

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.530
but nowadays, specifically
in high dense--

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:01.890
particle-dense environments,
you use solid-state tracking

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:02.520
detectors.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.120
And they're used in many, many,
many areas in particle physics.

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:09.000
The idea is to use
mostly silicon,

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.750
but you can use other
semiconductors as well.

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:14.970
You dope them, meaning
that you create

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:20.640
more holes by using p-doping
and n-type doping for more

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:21.910
electrons.

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:27.660
And then you bring n-doped and
p-doped materials together.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:30.510
And you-- you know, it's
the same way as in a diode

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:33.630
in that then you apply a
voltage in the opposite way

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:36.000
to create a very
large depleted zone.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.870
So in this depletion zone,
there is no electrons

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:41.669
and no holes available.

00:08:41.669 --> 00:08:43.530
But when a charged
particle travels through,

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:45.900
like is shown in
this picture here,

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:50.640
you create electron-hole
pairs, and they then

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.580
can be used in amplification
to create a signal.

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:56.410
That's the general concept in
which solid states or silicon

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:58.810
detector [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:00.520
The nice thing about
silicon detector

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:02.440
is that you can read
them out with very

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:04.380
fine spatial resolution.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:07.750
So from this 300
micrometers we just saw,

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:09.880
you go down to
several micrometers

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:12.100
in spatial resolution
when you measure particles

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:14.550
going through the detectors.

